        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer (The Distributed Idempotency Blueprint)
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        To prevent double-billing from duplicated webhooks, implement a two-stage Redis idempotency defense: verify the unique event ID using atomic <code>SETNX</code> with a 24-hour TTL, acquire a distributed Redlock across Redis nodes for critical state mutations, execute billing logic, and atomically commit the final processed state via Lua script.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Architecture Problem -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 space-y-6 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed mb-8">
      <p>
        Payment gateways such as <strong>Stripe, Adyen, and Paddle</strong> operate under an <strong>at-least-once delivery guarantee</strong>. If your server takes longer than 2,000–5,000ms to acknowledge an incoming webhook, or if a transient TCP timeout disconnects the gateway socket, the payment provider automatically retries the event.
      </p>
      <p>
        When two or more retry attempts hit a load-balanced cluster of API workers simultaneously, a classic <strong>read-then-write race condition</strong> occurs: both workers check the SQL database, find no completed invoice, charge the customer's payment method or provision duplicate credits, and write two duplicate records. Solving this requires distributed mutual exclusion using Redis.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-4">
        1. Webhook Retry Topology & Race Window Anatomy
      </h2>
      <p>
        When an upstream payment gateway dispatches a webhook, it places the payload into an HTTP request with a pre-configured timeout. If your application server does not return an HTTP 2xx code within that threshold, the gateway places the event into its retry queue with exponential backoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, intermediate network proxies, reverse load balancers (such as AWS ALB or Cloudflare), and container restarts can sever the client connection while the downstream worker is actively processing the database transaction. From the gateway's vantage point, the initial request failed. A retry request is immediately dispatched. If that retry hits a second worker pod while the first worker is still writing to PostgreSQL, duplicate business transactions occur unless blocked by a shared distributed lock.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-4">
        2. Two-Stage Idempotency Architecture: Fast Cache + Distributed Quorum
      </h2>
      <p>
        High-throughput payment systems cannot afford to acquire expensive distributed locks on every single incoming event. Instead, enterprise architectures deploy a <strong>two-stage filtering pipeline</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Stage 1 (Fast Filter):</strong> Query a fast in-memory Redis key <code>webhook:status:&#123;event_id&#125;</code>. If marked <code>PROCESSED</code>, immediately acknowledge with HTTP 200 without touching database pools or distributed locks.</li>
        <li><strong>Stage 2 (Mutual Exclusion Lock):</strong> If the key does not exist or is pending, acquire a short-lived Redlock token across a quorum of Redis nodes. This guarantees that only one worker executes the sensitive billing logic at any given millisecond.</li>
      </ul>

